# Copy to .env and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this template is not.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     $EDITOR .env
#
# THIS REPO IS PUBLIC. Never put a real key in this file — only in .env.
# A key that has ever been committed is burned even if the commit is reverted,
# because it existed in the history in that state. Rotate rather than rewrite.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required for Phase 1 (the toy harness) and any real capture
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your DeepSeek key. This exact name is what dsh reads by default —
# DEFAULT_API_KEY_ENV in packages/llm/llm-deepseek/src/index.ts:45 — so one
# variable serves both the toy harness and the specimen.
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-replace-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Capture proxy — leave these commented until you are actually capturing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Setting this routes every model call through the proxy. Leave it OUT of .env:
# if the proxy is not running, every request fails in a way that looks like a
# broken harness rather than a missing proxy. Export it in the shell for the
# one command you are capturing, so it disappears when that shell does:
#
#     export DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
#
# dsh resolves this through a LaunchEnvironmentSnapshot whose trust order is
# process > project-env > user-env, so a shell export beats any .env — which is
# what you want: the proxy stays in the path even if a .env says otherwise.
#
# DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787

# Proxy settings. Defaults are fine; override only if 8787 is taken.
# CAPTURE_PORT=8787
# CAPTURE_UPSTREAM=https://api.deepseek.com
# CAPTURE_FILE=campaigns/agent-harnesses/captures/session.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Using it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The toy harness (Phase 1) reads .env directly.
#
# dsh loads .env from the directory it is invoked in — which is the specimen's
# repo, not this one. Rather than keeping a second copy of your key there,
# export this one into the shell before running it:
#
#     set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#     cd ../deepseek-harness && pnpm dsh --profile headless "…"
#
# `set -a` marks the loaded variables for export, so they reach the child
# process; `set +a` stops that applying to everything else you type afterwards.
